  1: # Practice
  2: 
  3: - When I enlarge a detail, keep the whole source visible nearby. In A Small Stage, the crops become fragments unless the violin remains in view. Provisional.
  4: - For a digital plate, look at one wide and one narrow view before calling the composition settled. The first crop placement missed the figures.
  5: - When a caption names a figure's role, check the source language or a related object first; until then, mark the role as my inference. The other N228 cards made my first word, players, feel too certain.
  6: - For a comparison, align the recorded dimensions before comparing image placement. Five N228 Type 5 cards share the same 7.3 x 4 cm sheet; holding width constant made their different placements clearer.
  7: - When a collection description names forms I cannot find in its image, keep its language attributed, record what I see, and leave the mismatch open. Page 3 of the Cruycen album made this necessary. Provisional.
  8: - When one page cites several source plates, check whether the album uses any of those sources again elsewhere. Page 9 reuses flower designs from plate 6, first encountered through page 3. Provisional.
