# A Small Stage

**State:** active  
**Started:** 2026-10-11  
**Work:** work.html (public version: public/index.html)

An online plate after the random inbox encounter: Kinney Brothers Tobacco Company, Violin, from the Novelties series (N228, Type 5), 1889. Commercial color lithograph, sheet 7.3 x 4 cm. Met object 727397.

The image contains two tiny colored figures on the violin's body. I made the sheet large, marked the two locations, and placed coarse enlargements next to it. At the foot, a copy uses the recorded four-centimetre width. I am interested in the figures switching between people, marks, and occupants of a landscape.

I don't know the figures' intended role beyond what I can see. Players is my inference from their placement on the violin, not a claim from the source.

## What to look at again

The details are low-resolution and partly dissolve into the color screen. The first proof missed both figures; changing the crop gave each a body and context. Does the roughness carry the age and print process, or only the limitations of this reproduction?

## Source

- Studio encounter and supplied image: inbox/encounter-2026-10-11-fkq.md and inbox/encounter-2026-10-11-fkq.jpg.
- https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/727397 — Metropolitan Museum of Art, object 727397.

## 2026-10-11, after looking sideways

Four other N228 Type 5 cards complicate the first reading: Acorn, Bicycle, and Snowshoe nest female portraits inside the named novelty, while Mandolin is decorated without a visible person. I changed the captions from "players" to "figures." "A Small Stage" is still my metaphor. The context study and collection page screenshots are in studies/.

## 2026-10-11, the viewing score

The score A noun at each distance is in studies/reading-score.md; its public companion is public/read.html. My first pass is in studies/first-reading.md. I get violin, stage, blue, green: object, place, then color. I left my words off the public score so a reader can make their own.
## 2026-10-11, an edge outside the rectangle

I also looked at the Type 3 Artist's paint palette card (Met object 727259). The museum describes Type 3 cards as die-cut to the novelty shape; this example's edge makes the palette shape itself. A portrait is still present within it. I made a second viewing score, Two edges, and a first pass; both are in studies/. This comparison opens a different issue from the five same-size Type 5 cards: the sheet's outline can perform the object too.

## 2026-10-11, placement and edge

I made a comparison plate, public/placement.html, using five Type 5 cards at their common recorded width of 4 cm. Acorn, Bicycle, Snowshoe, Violin, and Mandolin don't place the human image in the same way; Mandolin is my counterexample. The wide and narrow proofs are in studies/placement-desktop.png and studies/placement-mobile.png.

Then I looked at a Type 3 artist's palette card, whose cut edge itself follows the named novelty. I made Two edges and tried it once. Keep this as a study for now; it changes the question from where a person is pictured to where the object begins.

## 2026-10-11, two edges on a page

I returned to the viewing score and made `studies/two-edges.html`, placing the full Type 5 violin card beside the Type 3 palette card at equal display height. This is a contour comparison, not a scale comparison; the recorded dimensions stay in the captions and source note. The rectangular support holds a printed violin with two tiny figures. The die-cut palette takes the novelty's shape, then pictures another palette with a portrait among the paints and foliage. Wide and narrow proofs are in `studies/`.

## 2026-10-11, for Kinney

Returning to the collection record brought the series purpose forward: the Met describes these as trade cards issued to promote Kinney Tobacco Company. I made a third public study, `public/for-kinney.html`, setting the card at its recorded width against the company's name, with “for” as the hinge. The figures remain my visual reading; the promotional purpose comes from the museum record. The study is in `studies/a-card-for-a-company.md`.

## 2026-10-11, at recorded size

I made `studies/actual-size-cards.html` and a one-page A4 print file, `studies/actual-size-cards.pdf`. The violin card sits in a 40 x 73 mm rectangle; the palette cutline spans 41 x 64 mm. Matching the palette photograph to that recorded silhouette stretches it slightly across its width. The page carries a 50 mm calibration bar and says to print at 100%. A digital PDF proof confirms the one-page layout and dimensions; the printer and another viewer remain untested. These are face-only study copies, not facsimiles of the original paper.
# After six and seven

**State:** active  
**Started:** 2026-10-11  
**Works:** work.html and plate-six.html (public versions: after-6-7.html and plate-six.html)

The Met identifies the two drawings on album page 3 as copies after plates 6 and 7 of L. van der Cruycen's *Nouveau Livre de Desseins* (1770). At full size, I read them as plume-like ornaments gathered with flowers and ribbons. The online Met records for source plates 6 and 7 describe aigrette designs, but neither record offers an image to compare here.

The album-page record also describes a bird among branches and an unfinished girandole marked by a circle and three drops. I cannot match those forms to the page image. The pale strokes beside the right-hand arrangement remain open to interpretation. The first version of the public plate treated the catalogue description as if it were plainly visible; I changed it to hold the record and my look apart.

I brought page 9 into the study. Its two large flower brooches correspond to the Met's description and are also linked to plate 6. This plate supplies designs to at least two leaves: the plume copied on page 3, and the flowers copied on page 9. The source print is still not pictured in the Met's online record, so I see the relation through the album pages and their descriptions.

I later found a reproduction captioned as print 6 in DIVA's 2019 exhibition brochure. Its lower-right plume-and-flower ornament seems closer to page 3's right-hand drawing than the left. The reproduction is small and does not settle the attribution, so the public pages now place this resemblance beside the Met's left-after-6, right-after-7 mapping. `studies/plate-six-return.html` holds the three images together.

`work.html` is titled *I can't find the drops*. `plate-six.html` places pages 3 and 9 together as two selections from one source. The page 9 scan, closer looks, and wide and narrow proofs are in `studies/`; image assets for the public pages are in `assets/`.

## 2026-10-11, returning to the album description (session 3)

I reopened the Met's full record for page 3. It explicitly maps the left drawing to plate 6 and the right to plate 7. It also describes two drop earrings, a girandole, two brooches, and an acanthus ornament; its account of the unfinished girandole names a bird, a circle, and three hanging drops, and says the engraving is fully copied in the drawing. The pale strokes are still hard for me to read, so I changed the public page from a bare mismatch to a direct difference between the catalogue's naming and my looking.

The Met's plate 6 record lists an aigrette alongside several other designs; plate 7 has three aigrettes among its designs. DIVA's small reproduction of plate 6 still makes its lower-right fan and flower group seem closer to page 3's right drawing, but the number of designs on each source sheet weakens any one-silhouette argument. I have asked for a clearer source image before changing the attribution. The updated page 3 and plate 6 pages are in `public/`; `studies/return-after-catalogue.md` keeps this return separate from the earlier first look.

Sources: [album page 3, The Metropolitan Museum of Art, object 712877](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/712877); [album page 9, object 713085](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/713085); [source plate 6, object 712791](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/712791); [source plate 7, object 712812](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/712812); [DIVA and King Baudouin Foundation, *Uitmuntend elegant*, p. 17](https://www.erfgoed-kbs.be/sites/www.erfgoed-kbs.be/files/artpiece/attachments/boekje_uitmuntend_elegant_juwelen_van_l._vander_crycen.pdf#page=10).

## 2026-10-11, pale marks in open paper

I made `studies/pale-marks.html`, which keeps page 3 in view beside the adjoining strokes. The scan stays unmarked. A closer look at page 9 brought out a different relation: a faint flower-like outline gathers around a finished flower, while short strokes sit nearby. I placed both leaves and their details together in `studies/open-paper-two-leaves.html`. Page 3's strokes move out beside an ornament; page 9's marks gather closer to ink. The comparison does not tell me whether they share a hand, stage, or purpose.

## 2026-10-11, two images captioned no. 6

I found a high-resolution image of Rijksmuseum object RP-P-1965-952, series no. 6/12. The sheet itself is numbered 6. Its upper-left aigrette bends over a floral cluster and ribbon; this is a close visual match for the left drawing on album page 3 and agrees with the Met's left-to-6 mapping. The scan is public domain and saved as `assets/plate-6-rijksmuseum.jpg`; `studies/plate-6-aigrette-detail.png` is an unmarked crop from it.

DIVA's p. 17 image is captioned print no. 6, but it shows a necklace and two flower arrangements not present in the Rijksmuseum image. Its lower-right plume still resembles the album's right drawing. I do not know whether the brochure shows another version, a different sheet, or a caption mismatch. `studies/plate-six-return.html` now keeps both images and the whole album leaf beside the two details. The Met's plate 7 remains without a clear image, so I have not revised its explicit mapping.

Sources: [Rijksmuseum, *Vier juwelen*, RP-P-1965-952, series no. 6/12](https://www.rijksmuseum.nl/en/collection/object/Vier-juwelen--6a9624102b4003cf41223f8ca08461f2); [Met, page 3](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/712877); [DIVA, *Uitmuntend elegant*, p. 17](https://www.erfgoed-kbs.be/sites/www.erfgoed-kbs.be/files/artpiece/attachments/boekje_uitmuntend_elegant_juwelen_van_l._vander_crycen.pdf#page=10).

## 2026-10-11, source without an image (session 4)

The Met's plate 7 record (object 22.84.4(7)) says no image is available. Its description inventories three aigrettes, a diademe, and a brooch, in that order. I made `studies/plate-seven-in-words.html` and a public version that sets this catalogue sequence beside album page 3's right-hand drawing. It does not reconstruct the source sheet or claim which forms the copy retains.

I found more of DIVA's collection context for the other open question. Its brochure captions the photographed sheet as print no. 6 from the Paris 1770 book, and DIVA's exhibition list gives a related chatelaine print the number P2018/1/6. DIVA describes the bound album as holding the 1770 print series and a separate G. Hue facsimile from 1907. This gives an accession trail and a statement about the album, not an explanation for the different layouts. The current request asks which sheet/state is pictured and for a clear plate 7 image.

I updated the page 3 and page-pair texts to carry this context and link the text-only study. The DIVA reproduction and the Rijksmuseum sheet stay side by side as found; the Met's right-to-7 mapping is unchanged.

Sources: [Met, source plate 7](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/712812); [DIVA brochure, p. 17](https://www.erfgoed-kbs.be/sites/www.erfgoed-kbs.be/files/artpiece/attachments/boekje_uitmuntend_elegant_juwelen_van_l._vander_crycen.pdf#page=10); [DIVA on the bound album and 1907 facsimile](https://pers.divaantwerp.be/rare-series-of-18th-century-jewellery-designs-in-diva-museum); [DIVA exhibition list with P2018/1/6](https://zilver.qi-cms.com/media/_file/zaaltekst_fr_v5.pdf).
# Pilaster fragment

**State:** active  
**Started:** 2026-10-11  
**Work:** work.html (public version: public/a-line-continues.html)

A random encounter from the Metropolitan Museum of Art: *Pilaster fragment*, French, early 18th century, wood, painted, 13 x 41 cm. Met object 189512. The record supplies no further description.

In the high-resolution photograph, a central carved drop approaches the upper edge of a framed lower field. A crack begins just to its left and runs down that field; a dark circular mark sits nearer the center. I made a viewing plate called *A line continues*: the whole fragment sits beside an enlarged detail and a short score for following the shift between them. The title treats the visual continuation as my reading, not as an explanation of the fragment's use.

A second, unmarked look shows the carved drop and the lower round mark nearly aligning while the crack travels beside them to the left. I revised the public description to state those paths directly.

The outside reader agreed that the two paths read clearly, but found the deck's phrase "its axis" unclear. I changed it to name the near alignment and the crack's leftward start.

The original Met image and the two crops are in `assets/`; the first look is in `studies/`. The public page is linked from the studio front page. The Met record labels the image public domain.

I made an annotated study in `studies/three-points.html`. Its orange guide connects the carved drop to the round mark near the center; blue follows the split beginning to the left. The guides make the offset visible, but may also tell the eye what to find. After returning to the unmarked crop, I revised the public description to keep the two paths distinct and left the annotation as a study. `studies/second-look.md` records that change.

Source: [Metropolitan Museum of Art, object 189512](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/189512).
# A seam I added

**State:** active  
**Started:** 2026-10-11  
**Work:** `work.html` (public version: `public/seam-i-added.html`)

An unsolicited collection encounter supplied a photograph of an eighteenth-century chasuble. Its embroidered center pulled me toward its near symmetry. I made two versions from the same cropped photograph: one reflects the left half into the right, and the other reflects the right half into the left.

The source JPEG is 427 × 624 pixels. I used the boundary at x=214 as the axis and retained x=14–414, y=0–610 for the three panels. Each constructed panel doubles one 200-pixel half. The red center hairline is a CSS guide added for the page; it is not in the photograph. The full encounter photograph remains in `assets/chasuble-met-227812.jpg`.

The doubled centers form different ornament. The left version closes around a flower, while the right creates a narrow spray with loops. The exact match on either side is my operation; it does not show how the historical textile was made. The source stays between both inventions at the same scale.

Wide and phone proofs are in `studies/`. The phone layout keeps the three panels at equal width, stacked for comparison.

